.TH SHARDPOSTCTL-MESSAGE 1 "2026-09-20" "ShardPost 0.23.0" "ShardPost Manual"
.SH NAME
shardpostctl-message \- list, search, show, export, flag, copy and move messages
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B shardpostctl messages
.RB [ \-after-uid
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-limit
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account mailbox
.br
.B shardpostctl search
.RI [ criteria ]
.RB [ \-limit
.IR n ]
.RB [ \-after
.IR cursor ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account
.br
.B shardpostctl message show
.RB [ \-uidvalidity
.IR v ]
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I account mailbox uid
.br
.B shardpostctl message export
.RB [ \-uidvalidity
.IR v ]
.I account mailbox uid
.br
.B shardpostctl flags
.RB [ \-add
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-remove
.IR list ]
.RB [ \-unchanged-since
.IR modseq ]
.RB [ \-uidvalidity
.IR v ]
.I account mailbox uids
.br
.B shardpostctl copy
.RB [ \-uidvalidity
.IR v ]
.I account mailbox uids destination
.br
.B shardpostctl move
.RB [ \-uidvalidity
.IR v ]
.I account mailbox uids destination
.SH DESCRIPTION
These are the support tools for single messages. A message is addressed
by mailbox, UIDVALIDITY and UID, so a mailbox that was deleted and
recreated in the meantime cannot answer with a different message: the
server refuses with exit code 65.
.PP
Without
.B \-uidvalidity
the client reads the mailbox's current value first. That is convenient,
but it only protects you against a change between the client's two
requests. When it matters, pass the value you saw in an earlier listing.
.PP
.I uids
is a comma-separated list, for example
.IR 42 ", " "42,43,50" .
UIDs that no longer exist are reported as
.I missing
instead of failing the whole request.
.SS messages
Message metadata of one mailbox in UID order: UID, size, internal date,
MODSEQ and flags. No headers, no content, so the role
.B diagnose
is enough. Page with
.BR \-after-uid .
.SS search
Searches one mailbox
.RI ( \-mailbox )
or the whole account. Criteria are combined with AND:
.TP
.BI \-from " text"
.TQ
.BI \-to " text"
.TQ
.BI \-subject " text"
Case-insensitive substring of the envelope.
.B \-to
also matches Cc.
.TP
.BI \-message-id " id"
Exact Message-ID, with or without angle brackets.
.TP
.BI \-text " words"
Full-text search over headers and body. This needs the full-text index;
while it is being rebuilt, or if it was never built, the search finds
nothing.
.TP
.BI \-since " date"
.TQ
.BI \-before " date"
Internal date, as
.I 2026-09-01
or RFC 3339.
.TP
.BI \-flag " list"
.TQ
.BI \-not-flag " list"
All of these flags are set, or none of them is.
.PP
A search examines at most
.I admin.search_scan_limit
messages (50000 by default) and returns at most
.B \-limit
hits (50 by default, 200 at most). If it stops early it prints a cursor;
pass it to
.B \-after
to continue. The audit log records which criteria were used, never their
values. Role:
.BR content .
.SS message show
Envelope and header section of one message, with its flags, size, dates
and MODSEQ. The body is not printed. Role:
.BR content .
.SS message export
Writes the message as stored (RFC 5322) to standard output. Redirect it
into a file to hand it to someone or to feed it into another tool.
Role:
.BR content .
.SS flags
Adds and removes flags, like IMAP STORE.
.B \-add
and
.B \-remove
take comma-separated lists; system flags are written as
.IR \eSeen ", " \eAnswered ", " \eFlagged ", " \eDeleted ", " \eDraft ,
keywords as themselves
.RI ( $Junk ", " $NotJunk ", " $Forwarded ).
.I \eRecent
cannot be set.
.B \-unchanged-since
skips every message whose MODSEQ is higher, which is the CONDSTORE rule:
those UIDs come back as
.I modified
and keep their flags.
Role:
.BR support .
.SS copy, move
Copies or moves messages into another mailbox of the same account. The
result maps every source UID to its new UID, as IMAP UIDPLUS does.
.B move
removes the messages from the source mailbox in the same step.
Role:
.BR support .
.PP
Deleting messages for good is not done here; it is a planned job, see
.BR shardpostctl-job (1).
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-uidvalidity " v"
UIDVALIDITY the UIDs belong to. Default: the mailbox's current one.
.TP
.BI \-limit " n"
Messages per page (messages: up to 1000) or hits per page (search: up to
200).
.TP
.BI \-after-uid " n"
Continue a message listing after this UID.
.TP
.BI \-after " cursor"
Continue a search at the cursor the previous page printed.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print the answer as JSON.
.SH EXAMPLES
What is in the INBOX, and since when?
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl messages -limit 20 user@example.org INBOX
.RE
.fi
.PP
"I never got the invoice from September": find it, then look at it.
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl search -subject Rechnung -since 2026-09-01 user@example.org
shardpostctl message show user@example.org INBOX 933
.RE
.fi
.PP
Hand a message to someone as a file, exactly as it is stored:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl message export user@example.org INBOX 933 > 933.eml
.RE
.fi
.PP
A message was marked as spam by mistake: take the flag off and move it
back:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl flags -remove '$Junk' -add '$NotJunk' \\
    user@example.org Junk 42
shardpostctl move user@example.org Junk 42 INBOX
.RE
.fi
.PP
Mark a batch as read, but only if nothing changed since you looked
(MODSEQ from
.BR "shardpostctl messages" ):
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl flags -add '\eSeen' -unchanged-since 940 \\
    -uidvalidity 1789726963 user@example.org INBOX 930,931,932
.RE
.fi
.PP
Find everything still flagged deleted, then expunge it as a planned job:
.PP
.nf
.RS
shardpostctl search -mailbox Trash -flag '\eDeleted' user@example.org
shardpostctl plan -mailbox Trash -deleted expunge user@example.org
shardpostctl apply -wait <plan-id>
.RE
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Done.
.TP
.B 65
The UIDVALIDITY does not match: the mailbox is not the one you addressed.
.TP
.B 67
No such account, mailbox or message.
.TP
.B 73
The copy would exceed the quota.
.TP
.B 77
The caller lacks the role
.RB ( diagnose ", " content " or " support ).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR shardpostctl (1),
.BR shardpostctl-job (1),
.BR shardpostctl-mailbox (1),
.BR shardpostctl-sieve (1)
